1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,890
Imagine a freelance designer who has just
delivered a website.

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:05,810
The work is finished.

3
00:00:05,810 --> 00:00:08,113
The financial admin isn't.

4
00:00:08,113 --> 00:00:13,069
A payment still needs requesting, incoming
transfers need checking, and the money

5
00:00:13,069 --> 00:00:14,109
needs organizing.

6
00:00:14,109 --> 00:00:18,275
This is an illustrative scenario, not a
customer testimonial.

7
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:25,309
QuickBooks' 2026 report says fifty-nine
percent of surveyed small businesses had

8
00:00:25,309 --> 00:00:27,918
invoices overdue by at least thirty days.

9
00:00:27,928 --> 00:00:33,299
That is broader business research, not proof
that IncomeFlow makes clients pay sooner.

10
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:39,203
IncomeFlow connects the payment request with
its financial outcome.

11
00:00:39,213 --> 00:00:44,315
Create a request for your own vault, choose
the supported test token amount, and sign

12
00:00:44,315 --> 00:00:45,035
the details.

13
00:00:45,035 --> 00:00:49,711
The client can independently verify the
receiving vault and its current owner.

14
00:00:49,711 --> 00:00:52,755
No tokens move when the request is signed.

15
00:00:55,098 --> 00:00:58,755
Payment Passport keeps the job and payment
evidence together.

16
00:00:58,765 --> 00:01:02,621
A regular token transfer has no invoice
identifier.

17
00:01:02,621 --> 00:01:07,984
So the owner explicitly signs an association
to the exact transaction and transfer

18
00:01:07,984 --> 00:01:08,245
log.

19
00:01:08,245 --> 00:01:12,623
Here, a genuine forty-token test payment
leaves sixty outstanding.

20
00:01:12,623 --> 00:01:16,354
A second sixty-token payment settles the
requested amount.

21
00:01:16,354 --> 00:01:21,400
Both are engineering tests using valueless
ifUSD, not commercial income.

22
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:24,247
Records can be exported and recovered.

23
00:01:25,092 --> 00:01:28,330
The financial action is performed by
Reactive Omni.

24
00:01:28,340 --> 00:01:32,588
An ordinary supported token payment reaches
the existing user-owned vault.

25
00:01:32,588 --> 00:01:37,568
The Reactive contract observes the exact
Transfer event and requests an authenticated

26
00:01:37,568 --> 00:01:38,095
callback.

27
00:01:38,095 --> 00:01:42,506
The vault checks the official provider and
its authorized Reactive sender, prevents

28
00:01:42,506 --> 00:01:46,120
duplicate payouts, and applies the owner's
current allocation rules.

29
00:01:46,120 --> 00:01:51,248
The two new test payments together
distribute fifty tokens, thirty tokens, and
twenty

30
00:01:51,248 --> 00:01:51,670
tokens.

31
00:01:51,670 --> 00:01:54,253
No backend wallet triggers the split.

32
00:01:55,086 --> 00:01:59,204
The Passport separately verifies the
incoming transfers, the owner's request

33
00:01:59,204 --> 00:02:01,807
associations, and the actual destination
payouts.

34
00:02:01,817 --> 00:02:06,458
Explorer links identify the original
payment, Reactive execution and successful

35
00:02:06,458 --> 00:02:06,987
callback.

36
00:02:06,987 --> 00:02:10,132
The older M1 proof remains a separate
demonstration.

37
00:02:10,132 --> 00:02:14,250
The illustrative job does not turn a
historical test into a client payment.

38
00:02:15,098 --> 00:02:17,266
The freelancer does the work.

39
00:02:17,276 --> 00:02:22,618
IncomeFlow helps organize the money, from
payment requested to income allocated, with

40
00:02:22,618 --> 00:02:24,127
a record you can verify.

41
00:02:24,127 --> 00:02:29,236
A testnet prototype, with owner control,
honest limits, and open-source proof.
